Minified UMD => \fRfast-equals/dist/min/index\&.js\fP
.PP

.PP
If you are having issues loading a specific build type, \fRplease file an issue\fP\&.
.SH "Available methods"
.PP
.SS "deepEqual"
Performs a deep equality comparison on the two objects passed and returns a boolean representing the value equivalency of the objects\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { deepEqual } from 'fast\-equals';

const objectA = { foo: { bar: 'baz' } };
const objectB = { foo: { bar: 'baz' } };

console\&.log(objectA === objectB); // false
console\&.log(deepEqual(objectA, objectB)); // true
.fi
.PP
.SS "Comparing \fRMap\fPs"
\fRMap\fP objects support complex keys (objects, Arrays, etc\&.), however \fRthe spec for key lookups in \fRMap\fP are based on \fRSameZeroValue\fP\fP\&. If the spec were followed for comparison, the following would always be \fRfalse\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const mapA = new Map([[{ foo: 'bar' }, { baz: 'quz' }]]);
const mapB = new Map([[{ foo: 'bar' }, { baz: 'quz' }]]);

deepEqual(mapA, mapB);
.fi
.PP

.PP
To support true deep equality of all contents, \fRfast-equals\fP will perform a deep equality comparison for key and value parirs\&. Therefore, the above would be \fRtrue\fP\&.
.SS "shallowEqual"
Performs a shallow equality comparison on the two objects passed and returns a boolean representing the value equivalency of the objects\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { shallowEqual } from 'fast\-equals';

const nestedObject = { bar: 'baz' };

const objectA = { foo: nestedObject };
const objectB = { foo: nestedObject };
const objectC = { foo: { bar: 'baz' } };

console\&.log(objectA === objectB); // false
console\&.log(shallowEqual(objectA, objectB)); // true
console\&.log(shallowEqual(objectA, objectC)); // false
.fi
.PP
.SS "sameValueZeroEqual"
Performs a \fR\fRSameValueZero\fP\fP comparison on the two objects passed and returns a boolean representing the value equivalency of the objects\&. In simple terms, this means either strictly equal or both \fRNaN\fP\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { sameValueZeroEqual } from 'fast\-equals';

const mainObject = { foo: NaN, bar: 'baz' };

const objectA = 'baz';
const objectB = NaN;
const objectC = { foo: NaN, bar: 'baz' };

console\&.log(sameValueZeroEqual(mainObject\&.bar, objectA)); // true
console\&.log(sameValueZeroEqual(mainObject\&.foo, objectB)); // true
console\&.log(sameValueZeroEqual(mainObject, objectC)); // false
.fi
.PP
.SS "circularDeepEqual"
Performs the same comparison as \fRdeepEqual\fP but supports circular objects\&. It is slower than \fRdeepEqual\fP, so only use if you know circular objects are present\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
function Circular(value) {
  this\&.me = {
    deeply: {
      nested: {
        reference: this,
      },
    },
    value,
  };
}

console\&.log(circularDeepEqual(new Circular('foo'), new Circular('foo'))); // true
console\&.log(circularDeepEqual(new Circular('foo'), new Circular('bar'))); // false
.fi
.PP

.PP
Just as with \fRdeepEqual\fP, \fBboth keys and values are compared for deep equality\fP\&.
.SS "circularShallowEqual"
Performs the same comparison as \fRshallowequal\fP but supports circular objects\&. It is slower than \fRshallowEqual\fP, so only use if you know circular objects are present\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const array = ['foo'];

array\&.push(array);

console\&.log(circularShallowEqual(array, ['foo', array])); // true
console\&.log(circularShallowEqual(array, [array])); // false
.fi
.PP
.SS "strictDeepEqual"
Performs the same comparison as \fRdeepEqual\fP but performs a strict comparison of the objects\&. In this includes:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Checking symbol properties
.IP "\(bu" 2
Checking non-enumerable properties in object comparisons
.IP "\(bu" 2
Checking full descriptor of properties on the object to match
.IP "\(bu" 2
Checking non-index properties on arrays
.IP "\(bu" 2
Checking non-key properties on \fRMap\fP / \fRSet\fP objects
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const array = [{ foo: 'bar' }];
const otherArray = [{ foo: 'bar' }];

array\&.bar = 'baz';
otherArray\&.bar = 'baz';

console\&.log(strictDeepEqual(array, otherArray)); // true;
console\&.log(strictDeepEqual(array, [{ foo: 'bar' }])); // false;
.fi
.PP
.SS "strictShallowEqual"
Performs the same comparison as \fRshallowEqual\fP but performs a strict comparison of the objects\&. In this includes:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Checking non-enumerable properties in object comparisons
.IP "\(bu" 2
Checking full descriptor of properties on the object to match
.IP "\(bu" 2
Checking non-index properties on arrays
.IP "\(bu" 2
Checking non-key properties on \fRMap\fP / \fRSet\fP objects
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const array = ['foo'];
const otherArray = ['foo'];

array\&.bar = 'baz';
otherArray\&.bar = 'baz';

console\&.log(strictDeepEqual(array, otherArray)); // true;
console\&.log(strictDeepEqual(array, ['foo'])); // false;
.fi
.PP
.SS "strictCircularDeepEqual"
Performs the same comparison as \fRcircularDeepEqual\fP but performs a strict comparison of the objects\&. In this includes:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Checking \fRSymbol\fP properties on the object
.IP "\(bu" 2
Checking non-enumerable properties in object comparisons
.IP "\(bu" 2
Checking full descriptor of properties on the object to match
.IP "\(bu" 2
Checking non-index properties on arrays
.IP "\(bu" 2
Checking non-key properties on \fRMap\fP / \fRSet\fP objects
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
function Circular(value) {
  this\&.me = {
    deeply: {
      nested: {
        reference: this,
      },
    },
    value,
  };
}

const first = new Circular('foo');

Object\&.defineProperty(first, 'bar', {
  enumerable: false,
  value: 'baz',
});

const second = new Circular('foo');

Object\&.defineProperty(second, 'bar', {
  enumerable: false,
  value: 'baz',
});

console\&.log(circularDeepEqual(first, second)); // true
console\&.log(circularDeepEqual(first, new Circular('foo'))); // false
.fi
.PP
.SS "strictCircularShallowEqual"
Performs the same comparison as \fRcircularShallowEqual\fP but performs a strict comparison of the objects\&. In this includes:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Checking non-enumerable properties in object comparisons
.IP "\(bu" 2
Checking full descriptor of properties on the object to match
.IP "\(bu" 2
Checking non-index properties on arrays
.IP "\(bu" 2
Checking non-key properties on \fRMap\fP / \fRSet\fP objects
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const array = ['foo'];
const otherArray = ['foo'];

array\&.push(array);
otherArray\&.push(otherArray);

array\&.bar = 'baz';
otherArray\&.bar = 'baz';

console\&.log(circularShallowEqual(array, otherArray)); // true
console\&.log(circularShallowEqual(array, ['foo', array])); // false
.fi
.PP
.SS "createCustomEqual"
Creates a custom equality comparator that will be used on nested values in the object\&. Unlike \fRdeepEqual\fP and \fRshallowEqual\fP, this is a factory method that receives the default options used internally, and allows you to override the defaults as needed\&. This is generally for extreme edge-cases, or supporting legacy environments\&.

.PP
The signature is as follows:

.PP
.PP
.nf
interface Cache<Key extends object, Value> {
  delete(key: Key): boolean;
  get(key: Key): Value | undefined;
  set(key: Key, value: any): any;
}

interface ComparatorConfig<Meta> {
  areArraysEqual: TypeEqualityComparator<any[], Meta>;
  areDatesEqual: TypeEqualityComparator<Date, Meta>;
  areErrorsEqual: TypeEqualityComparator<Error, Meta>;
  areFunctionsEqual: TypeEqualityComparator<(\&.\&.\&.args: any[]) => any, Meta>;
  areMapsEqual: TypeEqualityComparator<Map<any, any>, Meta>;
  areObjectsEqual: TypeEqualityComparator<Record<string, any>, Meta>;
  arePrimitiveWrappersEqual: TypeEqualityComparator<
    boolean | string | number,
    Meta
  >;
  areRegExpsEqual: TypeEqualityComparator<RegExp, Meta>;
  areSetsEqual: TypeEqualityComparator<Set<any>, Meta>;
  areTypedArraysEqual: TypeEqualityComparator<TypedArray, Meta>;
  areUrlsEqual: TypeEqualityComparator<URL, Meta>;
  unknownTagComparators: Record<string, TypeEqualityComparator<string, any>>;
}

function createCustomEqual<Meta>(options: {
  circular?: boolean;
  createCustomConfig?: (
    defaultConfig: ComparatorConfig<Meta>,
  ) => Partial<ComparatorConfig<Meta>>;
  createInternalComparator?: (
    compare: <A, B>(a: A, b: B, state: State<Meta>) => boolean,
  ) => (
    a: any,
    b: any,
    indexOrKeyA: any,
    indexOrKeyB: any,
    parentA: any,
    parentB: any,
    state: State<Meta>,
  ) => boolean;
  createState?: () => { cache?: Cache; meta?: Meta };
  strict?: boolean;
}): <A, B>(a: A, b: B) => boolean;
.fi
.PP

.PP
Create a custom equality comparator\&. This allows complete control over building a bespoke equality method, in case your use-case requires a higher degree of performance, legacy environment support, or any other non-standard usage\&. The \fBrecipes\fP provide examples of use in different use-cases, but if you have a specific goal in mind and would like assistance feel free to \fRfile an issue\fP\&.

.PP
\fI\fBNOTE\fP: \fRMap\fP implementations compare equality for both keys and value\&. When using a custom comparator and comparing equality of the keys, the iteration index is provided as both \fRindexOrKeyA\fP and \fRindexOrKeyB\fP to help use-cases where ordering of keys matters to equality\&.\fP
.SS "unknownTagComparators"
If you want to compare objects that have a custom \fR@@toStringTag\fP, you can provide a map of the custom tags you want to support via the \fRunknownTagComparators\fP option\&. See [this recipe]('\&./recipes/special-objects\&.md) for an example\&.
.SS "Recipes"
Some recipes have been created to provide examples of use-cases for \fRcreateCustomEqual\fP\&. Even if not directly applicable to the problem you are solving, they can offer guidance of how to structure your solution\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBLegacy environment support for \fRRegExp\fP comparators\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBExplicit property check\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBUsing \fRmeta\fP in comparison\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBComparing non-standard properties\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBStrict property descriptor comparison\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBLegacy environment support for circualr equal comparators\fP
